 "figures": {
  "heredity_novelty": {
   "title": "Like their parents, yet still changing?",
   "caption": "Each dot is a candidate world. Across: how much more a child acts like its parent than like a stranger, in the same situations. Up: how far the later eras of its line of descent lie from every earlier era, above the noise of playing one brain twice. A world in the top right passes on what its agents do and keeps changing it.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** every world of round 3 of the screen of random worlds that lived to the end of its run (Experiments 40, 41 and 42; 30,000 tokens, 3,000 iterations)\n\n**Data.** From each world's dossier, `GraphOfLifeRuns/.pilots/screen/candidates/<run>.json` (research/screen/candidates.py: the family tree from every birth, heredity in two kept worlds, the line of descent played in every kept world), its families, `.pilots/screen/families/<run>.json` (research/screen/families.py), and its health (research/screen/health.json), gathered by `research/screen/funnel.py` into `research/screen/candidates.json`.\n\n1. Read each candidate's heredity index (the mean of its kept worlds read) and late novelty from `candidates.json`.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py oeecandidates`."
  },
  "generations_population": {
   "title": "How many generations, and how many lines?",
   "caption": "Each dot is a candidate world. Across: the mean generation of the agents alive at the end (how many births separate them from the founders). Up: a rough effective population, from how fast the ancestry of the living narrows: the smaller it is, the more chance decides which line survives, and the larger a difference selection needs before it can see it.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** every world of round 3 of the screen of random worlds that lived to the end of its run (Experiments 40, 41 and 42; 30,000 tokens, 3,000 iterations)\n\n**Data.** From each world's dossier, `GraphOfLifeRuns/.pilots/screen/candidates/<run>.json` (research/screen/candidates.py: the family tree from every birth, heredity in two kept worlds, the line of descent played in every kept world), its families, `.pilots/screen/families/<run>.json` (research/screen/families.py), and its health (research/screen/health.json), gathered by `research/screen/funnel.py` into `research/screen/candidates.json`.\n\n1. Read each candidate's generations and effective population from `candidates.json` (`numbers`).\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py oeecandidates`."
  },
  "fertility": {
   "title": "Is having children passed on?",
   "caption": "Each dot is a candidate world: across every parent and child both born from iteration 100 to two thirds of the run, the correlation between how many children the parent had and how many the child had, less the same correlation with the children's counts shuffled among the children born in the same iteration. Above zero, families differ in how fertile they are, and selection has something to work on.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** every world of round 3 of the screen of random worlds that lived to the end of its run (Experiments 40, 41 and 42; 30,000 tokens, 3,000 iterations)\n\n**Data.** From each world's dossier, `GraphOfLifeRuns/.pilots/screen/candidates/<run>.json` (research/screen/candidates.py: the family tree from every birth, heredity in two kept worlds, the line of descent played in every kept world), its families, `.pilots/screen/families/<run>.json` (research/screen/families.py), and its health (research/screen/health.json), gathered by `research/screen/funnel.py` into `research/screen/candidates.json`.\n\n1. Read each candidate's `fertility` from `candidates.json` (families.py: correlation less the shuffled mean).\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py oeecandidates`."
  }
 }
}
